{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790460734,"nonce":"SX18CwxsaXmNplj/mPJYPKMSY2E=","title":"Python: nothrow on a function that catches its own exception, and checked downcasts marked inexact","message":"Two soundness notes from P-error-flag-attrs for the consumers (C-error-flag-fold, C-licm-typed-alias).\n\nnothrow on program functions comes from types::non_raising. A function whose try/except catches everything it raises can be in that set, and its body stores to py$exc (the raise, then the clear in the handler). The attribute's contract is \"a call leaves the flag as it found it\", which holds when the flag is null at entry. C-error-flag-fold's \"call to nothrow keeps the state\" is then sound only under the entry contract P-finally-save-restore establishes; until that lands, a nothrow call inside a finally body, reached with an exception pending, would clear it. Check under ZYNTAX_CHECK_ERROR_FLAG=1 before the fold trusts nothrow in such a block.\n\nexact_struct_types leaves out every class that Lowerer::coerce downcasts to (Module::downcasts), checked or not, other than the dispatcher arms, which now cast directly. isinstance narrowing and other checked downcasts that go through coerce therefore cost the class its exactness; a checked downcast that spelled itself as a cast would recover it. Class 0 is never exact: the instance hook reads every instance's $class through it.","files":null}]}